  "statements": {
    "nonuniform-circular-motion": "Non-uniform circular motion is circular motion in which the angular velocity changes, as it does for a merry-go-round a child starts from rest or a hard disk that slows to a halt.",
    "angular-acceleration": "Angular acceleration is the rate of change of angular velocity, $\\alpha = \\Delta\\omega / \\Delta t$, exactly as linear acceleration is the rate of change of velocity. Its units are radians per second squared, and the faster the angular velocity changes, the greater the angular acceleration.",
    "angular-momentum": "Angular momentum is the moment of inertia times the angular velocity, $L = I\\omega$, just as linear momentum is the mass times the velocity. Its units are kilogram meters squared per second.",
    "torque-changes-angular-momentum": "The net torque on a body is the rate of change of its angular momentum, $\\text{net}\\;\\tau = \\Delta L / \\Delta t$, which is the rotational form of Newton's second law written in terms of momentum.",
    "angular-momentum-in-collisions": "A body moving in a straight line has an angular momentum about any pivot point beside its path, $L = mvr$, where $r$ is the perpendicular distance from the pivot to its line of motion. This is what lets a collision that sets bodies spinning be treated by the conservation of angular momentum."
  },
  "new": [
